net: qrtr: ns: notify lookups even if announcing a local server failed

ctrl_cmd_new_server() returns early when broadcasting a newly registered
local server fails, which skips the loop that notifies pending lookups
about that server. On SC7180 (trogdor) this appears to leave the modem
waiting for a local service (rmtfs): the modem stalls during start-up and
its watchdog fires about 40 seconds later ("DOG detects stalled
initialization"). The Wi-Fi firmware service is hosted by the modem, so
ath10k_snoc does not get its QMI handshake until the modem happens to
restart without hitting the failure.

Notify lookups regardless of whether the broadcast succeeded, and print the
error code of the failed announcement. With this change the Wi-Fi of a
Lenovo IdeaPad Duet 3 (wormdingler) comes up on boot.

diff --git a/net/qrtr/ns.c b/net/qrtr/ns.c
index bcb090e..d88076b 100644
--- a/net/qrtr/ns.c
+++ b/net/qrtr/ns.c
@@ -488,13 +488,14 @@ static int ctrl_cmd_new_server(struct sockaddr_qrtr *from,
 
 	if (srv->node == qrtr_ns.local_node) {
 		ret = service_announce_new(&qrtr_ns.bcast_sq, srv);
-		if (ret < 0) {
-			pr_err("failed to announce new service\n");
-			return ret;
-		}
+		if (ret < 0)
+			pr_err("failed to announce new service: %d\n", ret);
 	}
 
-	/* Notify any potential lookups about the new server */
+	/* Notify any potential lookups about the new server, even if the
+	 * broadcast failed, so that remote nodes waiting on this service
+	 * (e.g. the modem waiting for rmtfs) are not left stalled.
+	 */
 	list_for_each(li, &qrtr_ns.lookups) {
 		lookup = container_of(li, struct qrtr_lookup, li);
 		if (lookup->service && lookup->service != service)
